    <div class="container" id="main-content">
        
        <!-- Introduction & History -->
        <section class="glass-card section-card" id="theory">
            <h2 class="section-title">📖 1. Introduction & History</h2>
            <p>Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of matter. Inside an atom, positively charged protons and neutral neutrons reside in the central <strong>nucleus</strong>, while negatively charged <strong>electrons</strong> revolve around the nucleus in fixed circular paths called <strong>shells</strong> or orbits.</p>
            
            <div class="timeline">
                <div class="timeline-event">
                    <strong>1913 - Bohr's Model:</strong> Niels Bohr proposed that electrons revolve in discrete orbits with fixed energy levels.
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-event">
                    <strong>1921 - Bohr-Bury Scheme:</strong> Niels Bohr and Charles Bury independently formulated the rules for the distribution of electrons in these different shells. This became known as the Bohr-Bury Scheme.
                </div>
            </div>
            
            <div class="note-box">
                <strong>📝 Note for UP Board:</strong> The arrangement of electrons in various shells of an atom of the element is known as its <em>Electronic Configuration</em>.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Rules of Bohr-Bury -->
        <section class="glass-card section-card" id="rules">
            <h2 class="section-title">⚖️ 2. The Bohr-Bury Rules</h2>
            <p>To write the electronic configuration, you must follow these golden rules:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Rule 1 (The 2n² Rule):</strong> The maximum number of electrons present in a shell is given by the formula <strong>2n²</strong>, where 'n' is the orbit number (energy level index) starting from the nucleus.</li>
                <li><strong>Rule 2 (Outermost Shell Limit):</strong> The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in the outermost orbit is <strong>8</strong> (Octet rule), even if it has the capacity to hold more.</li>
                <li><strong>Rule 3 (Penultimate Shell Limit):</strong> The second outermost (penultimate) shell cannot have more than <strong>18</strong> electrons.</li>
                <li><strong>Rule 4 (Step-wise Filling):</strong> Electrons are not accommodated in a given shell unless the inner shells are filled. That is, shells are filled in a step-wise manner.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color: var(--neon-cyan); margin: 20px 0 10px;">Maximum Electron Capacity Table</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Shell Name</th>
                        <th>Orbit Number (n)</th>
                        <th>Formula (2n²)</th>
                        <th>Maximum Electrons</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>K Shell</td><td>1</td><td>2 × (1)²</td><td>2</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>L Shell</td><td>2</td><td>2 × (2)²</td><td>8</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>M Shell</td><td>3</td><td>2 × (3)²</td><td>18</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>N Shell</td><td>4</td><td>2 × (4)²</td><td>32</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <!-- Valence Shells -->
        <section class="glass-card section-card" id="valence">
            <h2 class="section-title">🔋 3. Valence Shell & Electrons</h2>
            <p>The outermost shell of an atom is called its <strong>valence shell</strong>, and the electrons present in it are called <strong>valence electrons</strong>.</p>
            <p><strong>Importance:</strong> Valence electrons determine the chemical properties and valency of the element. Elements with completely filled valence shells (like Noble Gases with 8 electrons) are chemically stable and unreactive.</p>
            <div class="danger-box">
                <strong>⚠️ Common Mistake:</strong> Do not confuse <em>valency</em> with <em>valence electrons</em>. Valence electrons are the total electrons in the outer shell. Valency is the combining capacity (electrons gained, lost, or shared to complete an octet).<br>
                <em>Example:</em> Chlorine has 7 valence electrons, but its valency is 1 (it needs 1 electron to complete 8).
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Bohr-Bury vs SPDF -->
        <section class="glass-card section-card" id="comparison">
            <h2 class="section-title">🔄 4. Bohr-Bury vs Aufbau (SPDF)</h2>
            <p>While Bohr-Bury is perfect for basic understanding up to Calcium (Atomic No. 20), higher classes use the SPDF (Aufbau) principle.</p>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>Bohr-Bury Scheme</th>
                        <th>SPDF (Aufbau) Configuration</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Focus</strong></td><td>Main Energy Shells (K, L, M, N)</td><td>Subshells and Orbitals (s, p, d, f)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Rules</strong></td><td>Based on 2n² and Octet rules</td><td>Based on increasing energy levels (n+l rule)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Example: Potassium (19)</strong></td><td>2, 8, 8, 1</td><td>1s², 2s², 2p⁶, 3s², 3p⁶, 4s¹</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <!-- Interactive Generator -->
        <section class="glass-card section-card" id="generator">
            <h2 class="section-title">⚙️ 5. Interactive Configuration Generator</h2>
            <p>Enter any atomic number from 1 to 118 to instantly generate its Bohr-Bury electronic configuration and watch the atom build itself dynamically!</p>
            
